# Probability: Measuring Chance with Numbers

> Probability turns "probably" and "rarely" into precise fractions
> between 0 and 1 — favourable over total, plus honest counting.

## 1. Chance Has a Grammar

A **random experiment** has outcomes that cannot be predicted in
advance (a coin toss, a dice roll, a card draw). The **sample space**
lists every possible outcome; an **event** is any subset of it.

- One coin: the outcomes are H and T. One die: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.
- Two coins: HH, HT, TH, TT — four outcomes, not three (HT and TH
  are different paths to the same tally).

## 2. The Probability Formula

When all outcomes are **equally likely**:

@math label="eq:probability-def"
P(E) = \frac{\text{favourable outcomes}}{\text{total outcomes}}
@/math

Probability always lands between 0 and 1: a **sure event** scores
P = 1, an **impossible event** P = 0, and "even on a die" sits at 1/2.

## 3. The Standard Sample Spaces

| Device | Sample space | Size |
| --- | --- | --- |
| One coin | H, T | 2 |
| Two coins | HH, HT, TH, TT | 4 |
| One die | 1–6 | 6 |
| Two dice | ordered pairs (1,1) to (6,6) | 36 |
| Full deck | 4 suits × 13 cards (12 face cards, 4 aces) | 52 |

Suits: red **hearts** and **diamonds**, black **spades** and **clubs**
— 26 red and 26 black in all.

## 4. Two Dice: Count the Ways

Sum 7 arises from (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1) — six pairs.

@chart bar title="Ways to make each sum with two dice"
labels:
Sum 2
Sum 3
Sum 4
Sum 5
Sum 6
Sum 7
Sum 8
Sum 9
Sum 10
Sum 11
Sum 12

data:
1
2
3
4
