@/chart

@math label="eq:sum7"
P(\text{sum} = 7) = \frac{6}{36} = \frac{1}{6}
@/math

Sum 7 is the likeliest total; sums 2 and 12 have a single path each.

[example]
Two fair coins: what is the probability of **at least one** head?
The sample space is HH, HT, TH, TT; three outcomes contain a head, so
P = 3/4 — only TT fails the event.
[/example]

## 5. Cards, Carefully Counted

[example]
Draw one card. P(king or heart): 4 kings + 13 hearts − 1 king of
hearts (counted twice) = 16 favourable cards, so P = 16/52 = 4/13 —
naive addition double-counts the king of hearts.
[/example]

| Question | Favourable | Probability |
| --- | --- | --- |
| P(heart) | 13 | 1/4 |
| P(face card) | 12 | 3/13 |
| P(red ace) | 2 | 1/26 |
| P(king or heart) | 16 | 4/13 |

## 6. Combining Events

- **Complement rule:** P(not E) = 1 − P(E). At least one head in
  three tosses = 1 − P(all tails) = 1 − 1/8 = 7/8.
- **Addition rule:** P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B); the overlap
  vanishes for **mutually exclusive** events: P(king or queen) = 2/13.
- **Multiplication rule:** for **independent** events, P(A and B) =
  P(A) × P(B). Two heads = 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4.

Without replacement, independence breaks: P(two kings) = 4/52 × 3/51
= 1/221 — the first king is now missing.

## 7. Traps That Cost Marks

[warning]
Independent is not the opposite of mutually exclusive. Two coins
landing heads are independent — and both can happen. Mutually
exclusive events cannot both occur; independent events do not
affect each other's chances.
[/warning]

- "At least one" wants the complement: 1 − P(none); and ordered
  pairs matter — HT ≠ TH, so two-coin questions have four outcomes.
- 0 probability means impossible; a tiny probability still means
  possible — never confuse "unlikely" with "cannot".

## 8. Experimental vs Theoretical

**Theoretical probability** reasons from symmetry (each die face
1/6); **experimental probability** uses observed frequency — 52 heads
in 200 tosses estimates 0.26. The **law of large numbers** says the
experimental value settles toward the theoretical one as trials grow.

## 9. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| P(sum 7 with two dice)? | 6/36 = 1/6 |
| Two coins, at least one head? | 3/4 |
| P(king or heart)? | 16/52 = 4/13 |
| Two kings without replacement? | 4/52 × 3/51 = 1/221 |
